  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure continues to result in tranquil \nconditions today. \n\nBermuda high pressure off the Mid-Atlantic coast continues to\naffect the region today. A southerly pressure gradient will\ntighten as a cold front approaches from the west. Temperatures\nwill be warmer today compared to Monday with highs in the mid to\nupper 80s. Humidity levels will also creep up with surface dew\npoints rising into the mid and upper 60s. \n\nHigh pressure over the region today will shift\noffshore tonight and remain offshore through Wednesday. \nOverall, very pleasant and tranquil conditions are expected to \ncontinue through Wednesday.\n\nClear skies and light winds will prevail for the rest of today \nwith highs in the upper 70s to mid 80s. Temps tonight will be \nwarmer than the last couple due to a combination of light \nsoutherly flow and increasing high clouds keeping temps steady in\nthe upper 50s to upper 60s. Wednesday's highs will be a few \ndegrees warmer than those of today as southwesterly flow ramps \nup in the afternoon. Breezy conditions are expected with winds \ngusting up to 20-25 mph for inland locations and up to 25-30 mph\nnear the coast.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front will cross through the area on \nThursday bringing the potential for a round of showers and \nstorms.\n\nMid-level ridging will begin to break down on Wednesday across \nthe region as an upper-low traverses portions of the Hudson Bay \nand into Quebec. Thursday into Thursday night, a pronounced \nshortwave will pass through the region, with a cold front \ntracking from northwest to southeast across the region during \nthe evening into overnight hours. \n\nSome showers and a few embedded storms are expected late \nWednesday night into early Thursday morning for mainly the \nLehigh Valley, Poconos, and adjacent portions of northern NJ as \na decaying line of showers originating from the eastern Great \nLakes will be tracking toward the area. Some guidance is now \nbringing this activity as far southeast as the I-95 corridor by \naround daybreak Thursday. PoPs during this timeframe are \ngenerally 50-60% across the Poconos, 20-40% for the Lehigh \nValley and far northern NJ, and around 20% along I-95. \nElsewhere, conditions will likely remain dry. \n\nOn Thursday, the cold front will begin to approach the region \nby the afternoon and progress through the region into Thursday \nnight. The strongest shear (0-6km Bulk Shear exceeding 40 kts) \nlooks to remain across northern parts of the area and weaken \nwith southern extent. Meanwhile, destabilization looks modest at\nbest and confined to southern parts of the area. Given these \nfactors, the most likely scenario is scattered showers and \nstorms with very limited severe threat. However, some limited \nhydro concerns could evolve Thursday afternoon into Thursday \nnight across mainly Delmarva into southeastern PA and southern \nNJ. WPC has a Marginal (1 of 4) Excessive Rainfall Outlook over \nthese areas. Showers and storms look to clear southern parts of \nthe forecast area by daybreak Friday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Another disturbance will approach the area on \nSaturday night into Sunday causing another round for showers and\nstorms.\n\nBehind the front, a drier and more comfortable airmass looks to\nsettle into the region for Friday and into at least the start \nof Saturday as high pressure temporary settles in. \n\nBeyond the first half of Saturday though, the forecast becomes \na bit more uncertain as the front may stall off the southern \nMid- Atlantic coast or lift slowly northward towards the area \nwith waves of low pressure riding along it. If this is to come \nto fruition, there may be several periods of unsettled weather \nfor Saturday night into Sunday. At this point, the highest \nchances for showers and storms appears to be Saturday night with\nPoPs in the 40-60% range areawide.\n\nThe front looks to finally clear the area fully beyond Sunday, \nwith warm and dry conditions to start next work week.",
